diff --git a/docs/data-transfers.md b/docs/data-transfers.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..105e671
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/data-transfers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# Account exports and imports
+
+Admins can request a complete ZIP from **Account Settings**. Each person can
+request a ZIP of the cards currently accessible to them from their own profile.
+Both run in background jobs and email a signed download link. Links expire one
+day after the archive is ready; the storage URL returned by the link never lives
+past that deadline.
+
+Any signed-in identity can visit `/account/imports/new`, including identities with
+no account membership. Upload a complete account ZIP, not a profile/cards ZIP.
+An import creates a separate account and makes the importing identity its owner.
+Existing people are matched by normalized email address, or new identities are
+created. Other archived owners become admins. Original accounts are unchanged.
+Success emails link to the new account; failure emails explain the error.
+
+## Archive format (version 1)
+
+- `metadata.json` identifies the format, export kind, and record counts.
+- Account archives contain JSON records under their database table names. IDs are
+  portable references, not identifiers to overwrite in the destination database.
+- `cards/<id>.json` includes the card's description, comments, and attachment
+  filenames and paths. Account archives also keep comments and rich text as
+  individual records for restoration.
+- `files/<blob-id>` contains original attachment bytes. Original filenames,
+  content types, sizes, and checksums are in the blob records or card JSON.
+- Rich-text attachments and mentions use portable typed IDs instead of signed
+  IDs or host-specific download URLs. Import rebuilds these references.
+- Identity authentication credentials, sessions, access tokens, and transfer
+  archives are not exported. Account join codes and public board keys are newly
+  generated on import.
+
+ZIP64 archives stream to local disk or S3 multipart uploads. Import uses storage
+range reads instead of downloading an entire remote ZIP, and streams individual
+files to S3. The ZIP reader verifies paths, sizes, CRCs, and file checksums;
+individual JSON records are limited to 16 MiB and the ZIP directory to 128 MiB.
+
+Account restoration, people, relationships, rich text, attachment records, search
+indexes, and storage accounting share a database transaction. Normal domain
+callbacks are bypassed while restoring records, avoiding historical notifications
+and webhook deliveries. On failure, the transaction rolls back and newly uploaded
+files are removed. Duplicate job execution does not create another account.
+
+Run database migrations when deploying. Import uploads are global resources until
+an account exists, so Active Storage blob and attachment `account_id` columns are
+nullable. The authenticated import-upload endpoint marks uploads with their
+owning identity; another identity cannot claim these uploads.
